@* ================= Risk Analysis — FULL PAGE (stage 2, BRD §7.1) ====
    The scoring work, on a risk that is already in the register: category,
    likelihood and impact, plus the §19 approval gate — because this is
    the stage that produces a rating for a rating threshold to judge.

    WHY THIS IS A PAGE AND NOT A MODAL
    ----------------------------------
    It was a modal until 261-264 gave it three more things to carry: the
    practice/asset scope panel, the treatment-option picker, and the
    analysis-detail fields the procedure has always accepted. Inside an
    820px `pm-modal-wide` that is a scrolling box inside a scrolling
    page, with two-column content squeezed into whatever width was left.

    practice-view.cshtml (migration 139) made exactly this move for
    exactly this reason and its header says so in the same words. This
    page follows it: `pm-panel` per section, `pm-section-heading` on each,
    `pm-form-grid` for the fields, and the full `container-fluid` width
    the master layout already provides.

    IT IS NOT A NEW SCREEN KEY. It is a sibling of the tab panels above,
    shown by showAnalysisPage() and hidden by backFromAnalysisPage(), so
    it inherits the organisation, the scoring options and the employee
    list this partial has already loaded, and needs no menu row, no
    permission seed and no navigation round trip.
=================================================================== *@
<section id="riskAnalysisPageView" class="risk-page" hidden>
    @* The page's own heading bar, matching the partial's top-level one.
       The risk being analysed is the h1 — on a full page the record is
       the subject, not a line of metadata inside a form. *@
    <div class="pm-page-heading compact ra-head">
        <div>
            <p class="pm-eyebrow">Risk Centre / Risk Register</p>
            <h1 id="raPageTitle">Risk analysis</h1>
            <p id="raPageSubtitle">Score the risk, set its scope, and choose how it will be treated.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="ra-head-actions">
            <span id="raHeadStatus"></span>
            <button type="button" class="pm-button ghost" id="raBackBtn">
                <i class="fa-solid fa-arrow-left"></i> Back to register
            </button>
        </div>
    </div>

    <form id="riskRegAnalysisForm" class="ra-form">
        <input type="hidden" id="raRiskId" />

        @* ---- Row 1: what the risk IS --------------------------------
           STACKED, not beside the scoring card. These two were side by
           side so the analyst could read one while choosing the other,
           but context carries eleven read-only fields and scoring only
           three controls: in a 1.35fr column the context list fell to
           two narrow tracks and its values wrapped. Full width gives it
           three clean columns, and scoring is one short scroll below
           rather than off-screen. *@
        @* No .ra-row wrapper: a row holding one panel is an empty level.
           Panels sit directly in the form, exactly as Analysis detail
           below does, and .ra-form > * + * gives them the same 14px
           rhythm as every other row on the page. *@
        <section class="pm-panel">
            <div class="pm-section-heading">
                <h2>Risk context</h2>
                <p>Recorded at assessment. Read-only here — change it from the register.</p>
            </div>
            <dl class="pm-detail-grid" id="raMeta"></dl>
        </section>

        @* ---- Row 2: how it scores ----------------------------------- *@
        <section class="pm-panel">
            <div class="pm-section-heading">
                <h2>Inherent scoring</h2>
                <p>Likelihood &times; impact, against this organisation's own matrix.</p>
            </div>
            @* ra-grid-score, not ra-grid-1: .ra-grid-1 is still two
               columns for the residual panels that remain in a split
               row, and widening it would stretch those. *@
            <div class="pm-form-grid ra-grid-score">
                @* Category loses .full here. At two columns it had to
                   span to avoid a half-empty row; at three it sits
                   beside Likelihood and Impact, which is the row the
                   analyst actually fills in left to right. *@
                <div class="form-field">
                    <span>Risk category<span class="pm-required">*</span></span>
                    @* 375, 376: multi-select. pm-checkcombo, not the
                       risk-treat-options card grid Risk Type uses below --
                       risk_category_master is organisation-defined and
                       open-ended, so the searchable combo is the widget
                       that scales, and it keeps Category's seat in this
                       four-column score row instead of dropping to a
                       full-width band. Built by renderCategoryOptions()
                       in risk-centre.js (same render-fresh-with-checked-
                       baked-in convention every other pm-checkcombo
                       instance on this page already uses); its clicks
                       are wired through comboInHost(), widened to
                       recognise .risk-category-host alongside the
                       existing .risk-map-host. *@
                    <div class="pm-checkcombo risk-category-host" id="raCategoryCombo" data-checkcombo></div>
                </div>
                <label>
                    <span>Likelihood<span class="pm-required">*</span></span>
                    <select id="raLikelihood" required><option value="">-- select --</option></select>
                </label>
                <label>
                    <span>Impact<span class="pm-required">*</span></span>
                    <select id="raImpact" required><option value="">-- select --</option></select>
                </label>
                @* Fourth cell of the SAME row, not a full-width band
                   under it. The rating is the result of the three inputs
                   beside it, so reading left to right now gives
                   category -> likelihood -> impact -> rating in one
                   line, and the row of empty space this used to leave is
                   gone. It keeps .form-field (which carries the label
                   styling) but drops .full (which made it span). *@
                <div class="form-field">
                    @* Resolved from the organisation's own matrix, never
                       typed in. The server recomputes it on save. *@
                    <span>Inherent risk rating</span>
                    <div id="raRating" class="risk-rating-preview">Select likelihood and impact</div>
                </div>
            </div>
        </section>

        @* ---- Analysis detail ----------------------------------------
           Every one of these fields is already a parameter of
           sp_risk_register_assess and has been since 216. The modal asked
           for exactly one of them (consequence) because there was no room
           for the rest, so the other five were silently saved as NULL on
           every re-assessment. The page has the room, so it asks. *@
        <section class="pm-panel">
            <div class="pm-section-heading">
                <h2>Analysis detail</h2>
                <p>The justification behind the score. All of it is retained per version (BRD §20).</p>
            </div>
            @* Only Impact Analysis and Risk Type are asked for now. The
               other five controls are KEPT IN THE DOM, hidden, and are
               neither cleared nor defaulted:

                 * loadDetail() still fills them from the saved record;
                 * the assess payload still reads them with val(...);

               so a re-assessment round-trips whatever was stored instead
               of silently overwriting it with NULL. Deleting the markup
               would have made val() return "" and blanked five columns
               of sp_risk_register_assess on every save -- exactly the
               bug the comment above says 216 fixed.

               Give any of them back by removing the `hidden` attribute;
               nothing else has to change. *@
            <div class="pm-form-grid ra-grid-single">
                <label class="full">
                    <span>Impact Analysis</span>
                    <textarea id="raConsequence" rows="5" maxlength="4000"
                              placeholder="What happens if it materialises."></textarea>
                </label>

                @* Risk Type (313, 314) -- Confidentiality / Integrity /
                   Availability, MULTI-SELECT: a risk that leaks data and
                   corrupts it is Confidentiality AND Integrity, so a
                   single-choice control would force a false record. See
                   313's header.

                   Options come from the master table (loadRiskTypeOptions
                   in risk-centre.js), not hardcoded here -- the same
                   reason the Threats/Vulnerabilities pickers above read
                   their lists from the server. Same card-toggle markup and
                   CSS as Treatment option below (.risk-treat-opt), just
                   checkboxes instead of radios so more than one can be
                   on at once. *@
                <div class="form-field full">
                    <span>Risk Type<span class="pm-required">*</span></span>
                    <div class="risk-treat-options" id="raRiskTypeOptions"></div>
                </div>

                <label class="full" hidden>
                    <span>Risk description</span>
                    <textarea id="raDescription" rows="3" maxlength="4000"></textarea>
                </label>
                <label hidden>
                    <span>Risk cause</span>
                    <textarea id="raCause" rows="4" maxlength="4000"></textarea>
                </label>
                <label hidden>
                    <span>Existing controls</span>
                    <textarea id="raControls" rows="4" maxlength="4000"></textarea>
                </label>
                <label hidden>
                    <span>Process / activity</span>
                    <input type="text" id="raProcess" maxlength="200" />
                </label>
                <label hidden>
                    <span>Analyst remarks</span>
                    <input type="text" id="raRemarks" maxlength="4000" />
                </label>
            </div>
        </section>

        @* ---- Impact Details -----------------------------------------
           ABOVE Existing Controls, immediately below Analysis Details:
           what the risk impacts is read with the analysis that scored
           it, not with the practices that control it.

           Filled by the SAME riskMapping.mount() as the scope panel
           below, via impactHostId -- one /mapping call, one refresh, one
           save path. This host holds only the by-category table; the
           panel supplies the heading. *@
        <section class="pm-panel" id="raImpactPanel">
            <div class="pm-section-heading">
                <h2>Impact Details</h2>
                <p>
                    What this risk impacts &mdash; assets, vendors, people, teams and committees,
                    by Operationalize category.
                </p>
            </div>
            <div id="raImpactScope" class="risk-map-host"></div>
        </section>

        @* ---- Scope ---------------------------------------------------
           riskMapping.mount() fills this host. The panel supplies the
           heading, so the component is mounted with showHeading:false —
           otherwise the page would carry two headings for one section. *@
        <section class="pm-panel" id="raScopePanel">
            <div class="pm-section-heading">
                <h2>Existing Controls</h2>
                @* Five lines of prose above a section that is itself two
                   sections made the panel top-heavy before a single
                   practice was visible. One line; the rules it dropped
                   (every category, mapped once) are inherent behaviour,
                   not something the reader has to be told up front. *@
                <p>
                    Practices that control this risk, the dependencies each one brings in,
                    and what the risk impacts.
                </p>
            </div>
            <div id="raMapping" class="risk-map-host"></div>
        </section>

        @* ---- Treatment ----------------------------------------------- *@
        <section class="pm-panel">
            <div class="pm-section-heading">
                <h2>Treatment option</h2>
                <p>
                    Terminate, Treat or Transfer raises a Risk Treatment Task in Task Center owned
                    by the risk owner. Tolerate raises nothing and continues to Risk Acceptance.
                    Re-saving does not create a second task.
                </p>
            </div>
            <div class="risk-treat-options" id="raTreatmentOptions">
                <label class="risk-treat-opt">
                    <input type="radio" name="raTreatment" value="Terminate" />
                    <span class="t">Terminate / Avoid</span>
                    <span class="d">Stop the activity that creates the risk.</span>
                </label>
                <label class="risk-treat-opt">
                    <input type="radio" name="raTreatment" value="Treat" />
                    <span class="t">Treat / Reduce</span>
                    <span class="d">Apply controls to reduce likelihood or impact.</span>
                </label>
                <label class="risk-treat-opt">
                    <input type="radio" name="raTreatment" value="Transfer" />
                    <span class="t">Transfer / Share</span>
                    <span class="d">Insure, outsource or contractually share the exposure.</span>
                </label>
                <label class="risk-treat-opt">
                    <input type="radio" name="raTreatment" value="Tolerate" />
                    <span class="t">Tolerate / Accept</span>
                    <span class="d">Accept the risk as it stands, with a review date.</span>
                </label>
            </div>
        </section>

        @* ---- Action bar ----------------------------------------------
           Sticky to the bottom of the viewport. On a form this tall the
           alternative is scrolling to the end to save, then scrolling
           back to see the validation message that stopped it. *@
        <div class="ra-actionbar">
            <p class="pm-form-message" id="raMessage" role="status" aria-live="polite"></p>
            <div class="ra-actionbar-buttons">
                <button type="button" class="pm-button" id="raCancelBtn">Cancel</button>
                <button type="submit" class="pm-button primary">
                    <i class="fa-solid fa-floppy-disk"></i> Save analysis
                </button>
            </div>
        </div>
    </form>
</section>

@* ============ Residual Risk Analysis — FULL PAGE (258, 263) ========
    The SECOND score. Stage 2 above produces the inherent rating — the
    risk before anything was done about it. This produces the residual
    rating — what is left once the treatment has landed.

    Same three controls, deliberately: likelihood, impact, and a derived
    rating nobody types. Two scores that came out of two different
    methods would not be comparable, and the whole point of putting them
    in adjacent columns on the register is that they are.

    Only reachable on a risk that is Under treatment, Monitoring or
    Accepted, and only once an inherent rating exists — see errors
    56454-56457 in migration 258. The row menu disables the action for
    the same reasons, which is an affordance; SQL is the rule.

    WHY THIS IS A PAGE AND NOT A MODAL
    ----------------------------------
    It was an 820px `pm-modal-wide` and it carried everything the
    inherent analysis page carries: the scope panel, the treatment-option
    picker, three long-form fields and the §20 history table. Inside a
    fixed-width panel capped at `calc(100vh - 80px)` that is a scrolling
    box inside a scrolling page — the content compressed into a narrow
    column while the master layout's width sat unused on both sides.

    So it now uses the SAME `risk-page` shell as Risk Analysis and Risk
    Treatment: `pm-panel` per section, `pm-section-heading` on each,
    `pm-form-grid` for the fields, `ra-actionbar` at the foot, and the
    full `container-fluid` width the master layout already provides. Not
    one new class — the shell is shared precisely so a third page cannot
    drift from the two that came before it.

    IT IS NOT A NEW SCREEN KEY. It is a sibling of the tab panels, shown
    by showResidualPage() and left by backFromFullPage(), so it inherits
    the organisation and scoring options this partial has already loaded.
=================================================================== *@
<section id="riskResidualPageView" class="risk-page" hidden>
    @* The risk is the page's subject, so it is the h1 — on a full page
       the record is the heading, not a line of metadata inside a form. *@
    <div class="pm-page-heading compact ra-head">
        <div>
            <p class="pm-eyebrow">Risk Centre / Risk Register</p>
            <h1 id="rrPageTitle">Residual risk analysis</h1>
            @* No longer "confirm how the risk is carried from here" --
               that described the treatment-option radios this page used
               to end with. Scoring the residual risk IS the decision now;
               acceptance follows from it. *@
            <p id="rrPageSubtitle">Score what is left after the treatment. The risk then goes to Risk Acceptance.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="ra-head-actions">
            <span id="rrHeadStatus"></span>
            <button type="button" class="pm-button ghost" id="rrBackBtn">
                <i class="fa-solid fa-arrow-left"></i> Back to register
            </button>
        </div>
    </div>

    @* ---- The flow, stated before anything is asked for ---------------
       Inherent -> Treatment -> Residual -> Acceptance. The page is the
       third step of that story and it used to open in the middle of it:
       a score box with no answer to "a reduction from what, by what
       work?".

       The rail is not decoration. Each step carries the actual value —
       the inherent chip, the treatment option with its task counts, the
       residual chip as it is being chosen — so the arithmetic of the
       whole exercise is legible before the analyst scrolls. Rendered by
       renderResidualFlow(), and step 3 updates live as the selects
       change.

       Step 4 replaced the treatment-option radios this page used to end
       with. The analyst is told where the risk goes rather than asked:
       saving the residual score sets residual_pending = 0, which makes
       the stage AcceptanceDue by itself. *@
    <ol class="risk-flow risk-flow-4" id="rrFlow" aria-label="Inherent risk to residual risk to acceptance"></ol>

    <form id="riskResidualForm" class="ra-form">
        <input type="hidden" id="rrRiskId" />

        @* ================= STEP 1 — the risk, as it was ===============
           Read-only. Everything here was decided at analysis time and is
           changed from the register, not from this page. *@
        <div class="ra-row ra-row-split ra-step" data-step="1">
            <section class="pm-panel">
                <div class="pm-section-heading">
                    <h2><span class="ra-step-badge">1</span> Risk summary</h2>
                    <p>What the risk is. Recorded at assessment — change it from the register.</p>
                </div>
                <dl class="pm-detail-grid" id="rrMeta"></dl>
            </section>

            <section class="pm-panel">
                <div class="pm-section-heading">
                    <h2>Inherent risk <span class="pm-hint">before treatment</span></h2>
                    @* No numeric score row any more: the rating chip names the level and
                       Likelihood x Impact is what produced it, so the number was the same
                       fact a third time. Cause and Controls at analysis went with it --
                       the Analysis page stopped asking for them, so they render empty on
                       every risk analysed since. What the analyst actually wrote is the
                       Impact analysis field. *@
                    <p>The rating this residual assessment is measured against, on the same
                       matrix, and the impact analysis it was scored from.</p>
                </div>
                <dl class="pm-detail-grid" id="rrInherent"></dl>
            </section>
        </div>

        @* ---- Impact Details -----------------------------------------
           ABOVE step 2, and READ-ONLY.

           Position: what the risk impacts belongs with the assessment
           that scored it (step 1), not after the treatment that was
           done about it. So it sits between step 1 and step 2 rather
           than further down beside Existing Controls.

           Read-only: this page re-scores what treatment has left. What
           the risk impacts was established at Risk Analysis, and Review
           is where it is revised -- editing it mid-residual would change
           the thing being measured while measuring it. Mounted with
           impactReadOnly, so the scope panel below stays editable (a
           residual assessment may legitimately re-map practices) while
           this table renders as chips with no picker at all.

           Same host pairing as every other page -- one riskMapping
           mount, one /mapping call. *@
        <section class="pm-panel" id="rrImpactPanel">
            <div class="pm-section-heading">
                <h2>Impact Details <span class="pm-hint">read-only</span></h2>
                <p>
                    What this risk impacts &mdash; assets, vendors, people, teams and
                    committees, as established at Risk Analysis. Revised on the Review page.
                </p>
            </div>
            <div id="rrImpactScope" class="risk-map-host"></div>
        </section>

        @* ================= STEP 2 — what was done about it ============
           READ-ONLY, AND READ FROM TREATMENT'S OWN SOURCE.
           Every value in this section comes from sp_risk_treatment_state
           and the register row — the same two reads the Risk Treatment
           page makes, rendered by the same functions
           (refreshTreatmentState / taskRow, called with readOnly:true).
           There is no second copy of task state here and no editing:
           the button in the heading navigates to the Treatment page,
           which is where treatment work is actually done.

           The gate banner is the point of the section. A residual score
           entered while treatment tasks are still open is a score for
           work that has not happened, and the page must not let that be
           assumed — so the banner names the state in words, the tiles
           count it, and step 3 repeats the warning where the score is
           actually typed. *@
        <section class="pm-panel ra-step" data-step="2" id="rrTreatmentPanel">
            <div class="pm-section-heading ra-heading-row">
                <div>
                    <h2><span class="ra-step-badge">2</span> Risk treatment <span class="pm-hint">read-only</span></h2>
                    <p>The treatment already recorded for this risk, exactly as the Risk Treatment page holds it.</p>
                </div>
                <button type="button" class="pm-button ghost" id="rrOpenTreatmentBtn">
                    <i class="fa-solid fa-diagram-project"></i> Open risk treatment
                </button>
            </div>

            @* sp_risk_treatment_state returns both the answer and the
               reason, so this banner explains itself rather than being a
               colour. *@
            <div class="risk-gate" id="rrGate"></div>
            <dl class="pm-detail-grid" id="rrTreatmentMeta"></dl>
            <div class="risk-tiles" id="rrTiles"></div>

            <h3 class="risk-subhead">Treatment tasks <span class="pm-hint">from Task Center</span></h3>
            <div class="pm-table-wrap">
                @* Seven columns, not eight: the Actions column of the
                   Treatment page is absent because this view is read-only.
                   taskRow() omits it when called with readOnly:true rather
                   than rendering a disabled menu nobody can use. *@
                <table id="rrTaskTable">
                    <thead>
                        <tr>
                            <th>Task</th><th>Title</th><th>Owner</th><th>Priority</th>
                            <th>Target date</th><th>Status</th><th>Sub tasks</th>
                        </tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody id="rrTaskBody">
                        <tr><td colspan="7" class="pm-empty-row">Loading treatment work...</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>
        </section>

        @* ================= STEP 3 — what is left ======================
           The only editable step on the page. *@
        @* No .ra-row-split wrapper any more. That row was a two-column
           grid (1.35fr / 1fr) holding Residual risk beside Justification;
           with Justification gone it kept reserving the second column and
           left a third of the page blank.

           The panel now sits directly in the form, which is what this
           page already does for every single-panel step -- "a row holding
           one panel is an empty level", as the Risk context step above
           puts it. .ra-form > * + * supplies the same 14px rhythm, and
           .ra-step / data-step move onto the panel itself so the rail's
           scroll target is unchanged. *@
        <section class="pm-panel ra-step" data-step="3">
            <div class="pm-section-heading">
                <h2><span class="ra-step-badge">3</span> Residual risk</h2>
                <p>
                    Score the risk <strong>as it stands now</strong>, after the treatment above. The
                    rating and risk level are derived from the same matrix as the inherent rating,
                    so the two are comparable.
                </p>
            </div>
            @* Repeated here, where the score is actually typed: the
               banner in step 2 is three panels up by the time the
               analyst is choosing a likelihood. Hidden when treatment
               is complete -- a warning that is always on screen stops
               being read. *@
            <div class="risk-gate is-blocked" id="rrIncompleteWarning" hidden></div>

            @* ra-grid-residual, not ra-grid-1. .ra-grid-1 exists for
               panels still inside a split row and is deliberately left
               alone -- the Review page's two panels still use it. This
               panel is full width now, so it gets its own four-column
               track set and the fields stop being stretched two-up
               across the whole page. *@
            <div class="pm-form-grid ra-grid-residual">
                <label>
                    <span>Residual likelihood<span class="pm-required">*</span></span>
                    <select id="rrLikelihood" required><option value="">-- select --</option></select>
                </label>
                <label>
                    <span>Residual impact<span class="pm-required">*</span></span>
                    <select id="rrImpact" required><option value="">-- select --</option></select>
                </label>
                @* Rating and delta sit BESIDE the two selects rather than
                   spanning rows under them. They are the output of those
                   selects, so the whole calculation reads left to right
                   on one line: likelihood x impact -> rating -> movement.
                   As full-width rows they each wasted a line. *@
                <div class="form-field">
                    @* Resolved from the organisation's own matrix, never
                       typed in. The server recomputes it on save. *@
                    <span>Residual risk rating &amp; level</span>
                    <div id="rrRating" class="risk-rating-preview">Select likelihood and impact</div>
                </div>
                <div class="form-field">
                    <span>Change against inherent</span>
                    <div id="rrDelta" class="risk-delta">--</div>
                </div>
                @* THE JUSTIFICATION CARD IS GONE, and only this field
                   came across from it.

                   It used to ask for "Treatment carried out" and
                   "Controls now in place" as free text -- both of
                   which this page ALREADY SHOWS: the treated task
                   details are in step 2 above, read-only and straight
                   from the task records, and the controls are in the
                   Existing Controls panel below. Asking the analyst
                   to retype them produced a second, hand-written copy
                   that could disagree with the first, and the version
                   history then retained the disagreement.

                   The remark is different in kind -- it is the one
                   thing on that card that exists nowhere else -- so it
                   sits with the score it explains, spanning the row
                   beneath it. *@
                <label class="full">
                    <span>Analyst remark</span>
                    <textarea id="rrRemarks" rows="2" maxlength="4000"
                              placeholder="Anything the next reviewer should know about this score."></textarea>
                    <span class="pm-hint">
                        Optional. The treatment work and the controls are shown above and below &mdash;
                        no need to repeat them here.
                    </span>
                </label>
            </div>
        </section>

        @* ---- Scope ---------------------------------------------------
           The residual analysis is a FULL analysis, so it carries the
           same scope panel as the inherent one: a risk's practice/asset
           scope may have changed while the treatment was being done.

           riskMapping.mount() fills this host with showHeading:false —
           the panel supplies the heading, and two headings for one
           section is what a copied component looks like. *@
        <section class="pm-panel" id="rrScopePanel">
            <div class="pm-section-heading">
                <h2>Existing Controls</h2>
                <p>
                    Adjust the scope if the treatment changed which practices this risk reaches.
                </p>
            </div>
            <div id="rrMapping" class="risk-map-host"></div>
        </section>

        @* ---- Treatment option: REMOVED -------------------------------
           This page used to end with a "Treatment option after treatment"
           radio set, so the analyst had to make a treatment decision
           again after scoring the residual risk.

           It no longer does, and nothing replaces it. Once the residual
           risk is assessed, the risk goes to Risk Acceptance -- that is
           the only sensible next step at this point in the flow, so
           asking for it was asking the analyst to confirm the obvious,
           with four ways to get it wrong.

           HOW THE ROUTING HAPPENS NOW, with no code deciding it here:
           sp_risk_residual_analysis_save sets residual_pending = 0, and
           vw_pm_risk_workflow_stage then reads the risk as AcceptanceDue
           -- treatment tasks are closed (56574 refuses the save
           otherwise), the residual is assessed, and it has not been
           accepted yet. It appears on the Accept Risk tab by itself.

           The procedure still ACCEPTS @treatment_option_code, and the
           API still carries it, so an integration that genuinely needs to
           change the option can. This page simply stops asking.

           A risk that really does need another round of treatment is a
           different decision, and it belongs where treatment decisions
           are made -- the Risk Treatment page -- not tacked onto the end
           of a scoring form. *@

        @* ---- Acceptance / decision -----------------------------------
           READ-ONLY, like step 2, and for the same reason: acceptance is
           written by sp_risk_acceptance_save through its own modal, and a
           second set of acceptance inputs here would be a second way to
           write one decision. What this shows is the decision as it
           stands -- who accepted, when, and when the risk comes back --
           because "is this residual level already accepted, and until
           when?" is part of the picture the analyst is completing.

           The action is the one that already exists: "Save & accept
           risk" in the bar below, which saves this analysis and then
           opens that same modal. *@
        <section class="pm-panel">
            <div class="pm-section-heading">
                <h2>Acceptance &amp; next review</h2>
                <p>The acceptance decision as it stands. Recorded through Risk Acceptance — shown here for context.</p>
            </div>
            <dl class="pm-detail-grid" id="rrAcceptance"></dl>
        </section>

        @* ---- History -------------------------------------------------
           BRD §20 — every retained residual version, same table shape as
           the candidate's analysis history. On a page it gets the full
           width, so the eight columns stop wrapping. *@
        <section class="pm-panel">
            <div class="pm-section-heading">
                <h2>Residual history</h2>
                <p>Every retained version, newest first, each carrying the inherent rating as it stood at the time.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="pm-table-wrap" id="rrHistory"></div>
        </section>

        @* ---- Action bar ----------------------------------------------
           Sticky, like the analysis page's: on a form this tall the
           alternative is scrolling to the end to save, then back to read
           the validation message that stopped it.

           "Save & accept risk" SAVES FIRST, then opens the acceptance
           modal -- accepting a risk while an unsaved residual score sits
           on screen would record an acceptance of a number that was
           never persisted. *@
        <div class="ra-actionbar">
            <p class="pm-form-message" id="rrMessage" role="status" aria-live="polite"></p>
            <div class="ra-actionbar-buttons">
                <button type="button" class="pm-button" id="rrCancelBtn">Cancel</button>
                <button type="button" class="pm-button" id="rrAcceptBtn">
                    <i class="fa-solid fa-circle-check"></i> Save &amp; accept risk
                </button>
                <button type="submit" class="pm-button primary">
                    <i class="fa-solid fa-floppy-disk"></i> Save residual analysis
                </button>
            </div>
        </div>
    </form>
</section>

@* ================= Review Risk — FULL PAGE (264) ====================
    Reassessing a risk whose review date has arrived.

    THE SAME FORM AS RISK ANALYSIS, ON PURPOSE.
    Original requirement: "Open a page similar to the existing Risk
    Analysis page ... reassess the risk using the same analysis
    process." Not similar -- the same. The server delegates to
    sp_risk_register_assess, the exact procedure the analysis screen
    calls, so a future change to how risks are analysed changes reviews
    too and the two cannot drift. The two extra fields are the review's
    own: what changed, and when to look again.

    Was a pm-modal-wide. It carried the same load as the residual page —
    a full reassessment, the scope panel, the treatment options and a
    next-review date — and inside a modal the scope panel's checkcombo
    menus were clipped and the form scrolled inside a box that was
    itself scrolling.

    Same shell as Residual analysis, deliberately: pm-page-heading with
    the risk as the h1, a risk-flow rail, pm-panel per section,
    ra-actionbar at the foot. A reviewer moving between the two screens
    should not have to relearn where anything is.

    THREE flow steps, not four. .risk-flow is a five-track grid, so
    3 steps + 2 arrows fills it exactly; a fourth step pushes the rail
    to seven items and it overflows.

    NO history section here, unlike the residual page. There is no
    review-history read in the API — sp_risk_review_perform writes, and
    sp_risk_review_due_list lists what is due. Inventing a panel with
    nothing behind it would be worse than not having one.
*@
<section id="riskReviewPageView" class="risk-page" hidden>
    <div class="pm-page-heading compact ra-head">
        <div>
            <p class="pm-eyebrow">Risk Centre / Review</p>
            <h1 id="rvPageTitle">Review risk</h1>
            <p id="rvPageSubtitle">Reassess the risk as it stands today, and say where it goes from here.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="ra-head-actions">
            <span id="rvHeadStatus"></span>
            <button type="button" class="pm-button ghost" id="rvBackBtn">
                <i class="fa-solid fa-arrow-left"></i> Back to register
            </button>
        </div>
    </div>

    @* Where this review stands: what was decided last time, what is
       being entered now, and where saving will land. Step 3 updates as
       the treatment option changes, so the routing is stated before the
       reviewer commits to it rather than surprising them afterwards. *@
    <ol class="risk-flow" id="rvFlow" aria-label="Last assessment to next step"></ol>

    <form id="riskReviewForm" class="ra-form">
        <input type="hidden" id="rvRiskId" />

        @* ---- STEP 1 — the risk as it stands (read-only) -------------- *@
        <div class="ra-row ra-row-split ra-step" data-step="1">
            <section class="pm-panel">
                <div class="pm-section-heading">
                    <h2><span class="ra-step-badge">1</span> Risk summary</h2>
                    <p>What the risk is, as the register holds it today.</p>
                </div>
                <dl class="pm-detail-grid" id="rvMeta"></dl>
            </section>

            <section class="pm-panel">
                <div class="pm-section-heading">
                    <h2>Where it stands <span class="pm-hint">before this review</span></h2>
                    <p>The scores and the decision this review is revisiting.</p>
                </div>
                <dl class="pm-detail-grid" id="rvStanding"></dl>
            </section>
        </div>

        @* ---- STEP 2 — the reassessment (the editable step) -----------
           No .ra-row-split wrapper. That row was a two-column grid
           holding Reassessment beside Justification; with Justification
           gone it would keep reserving the second column and leave a
           third of the page blank. The panel sits directly in the form,
           the way every single-panel step on these pages does. *@
        <section class="pm-panel ra-step" data-step="2">
            <div class="pm-section-heading">
                <h2><span class="ra-step-badge">2</span> Reassessment</h2>
                <p>
                    Score the risk <strong>as it stands now</strong>. Pre-filled with the current
                    assessment, so only what has actually moved needs changing. The rating is
                    resolved from the organisation's matrix, never typed.
                </p>
            </div>
            @* ra-grid-score, the same class the Inherent scoring panel
               uses: category, likelihood, impact and the resolved rating
               are exactly the same four fields in the same order, so they
               get the same four-column row rather than a second class
               that would have to be kept in step with it. *@
            <div class="pm-form-grid ra-grid-score">
                <label>
                    <span>Risk category<span class="pm-required">*</span></span>
                    <select id="rvCategory" required><option value="">-- select --</option></select>
                </label>
                <label>
                    <span>Likelihood<span class="pm-required">*</span></span>
                    <select id="rvLikelihood" required><option value="">-- select --</option></select>
                </label>
                <label>
                    <span>Impact<span class="pm-required">*</span></span>
                    <select id="rvImpact" required><option value="">-- select --</option></select>
                </label>
                <div class="form-field">
                    <span>Reassessed inherent rating &amp; level</span>
                    <div id="rvRating" class="risk-rating-preview">Select likelihood and impact</div>
                </div>
                @* THE JUSTIFICATION CARD IS GONE, and only this field came
                   across from it.

                   It also asked for "Potential consequence / impact" and
                   "Controls now in place" as free text. The controls are
                   already on this page, in the Existing Controls panel
                   below, and the treated task details are shown above --
                   retyping them produced a second copy that could
                   contradict the first.

                   IMPORTANT, and the reason this is not simply a
                   deletion: sp_risk_analysis_save INSERTS A NEW ANALYSIS
                   VERSION from whatever it is handed, and that row becomes
                   is_current. Dropping the two fields from the request
                   would therefore blank potential_consequence and
                   existing_controls on the live assessment. The client
                   still sends both -- carried forward unchanged from the
                   risk as loaded -- so the record keeps them and nobody
                   has to retype them. See onReviewSubmit. *@
                <label class="full">
                    <span>Analyst remark</span>
                    <textarea id="rvRemarks" rows="2" maxlength="4000"
                              placeholder="What has changed since the last assessment?"></textarea>
                    <span class="pm-hint">
                        Optional. The treated tasks and the existing controls are already shown
                        on this page &mdash; no need to repeat them here.
                    </span>
                </label>
            </div>
        </section>

        @* ---- Impact Details -----------------------------------------
           Above Existing Controls, same position and same host pairing
           as the other pages. Editable: a review is where a change in
           what the risk reaches is most likely to be noticed. *@
        <section class="pm-panel" id="rvImpactPanel">
            <div class="pm-section-heading">
                <h2>Impact Details</h2>
                <p>
                    What this risk impacts &mdash; assets, vendors, people, teams and committees,
                    by Operationalize category. Adjust it if the review found otherwise.
                </p>
            </div>
            <div id="rvImpactScope" class="risk-map-host"></div>
        </section>

        @* ---- Scope --------------------------------------------------
           Same panel, same heading and same host shape as the residual
           page's. A review is where a scope change is most likely to be
           noticed, so it is editable here exactly as it was in the
           modal this replaces. *@
        <section class="pm-panel" id="rvScopePanel">
            <div class="pm-section-heading">
                <h2>Existing Controls</h2>
                <p>Adjust the scope if this risk now reaches different practices or assets.</p>
            </div>
            <div id="rvMapping" class="risk-map-host"></div>
        </section>

        @* ---- Treatment option ---------------------------------------
           The option decides where saving lands, so each choice says so.
           Descriptions are filled in rather than left as spacers, to
           match the residual page's option list. *@
        <section class="pm-panel">
            <div class="pm-section-heading">
                <h2>Treatment option</h2>
                <p>
                    Optional. Leave on <em>No change</em> to keep the decision already recorded.
                    Tolerate takes this risk on to acceptance; the other three send it back to
                    treatment, and its residual score is assessed once that work is done.
                </p>
            </div>
            <div class="risk-treat-options" id="rvTreatmentOptions">
                <label class="risk-treat-opt">
                    <input type="radio" name="rvTreatment" value="" checked />
                    <span class="t">No change</span>
                    <span class="d">Keep the treatment decision already recorded.</span>
                </label>
                <label class="risk-treat-opt">
                    <input type="radio" name="rvTreatment" value="Terminate" />
                    <span class="t">Terminate / Avoid</span>
                    <span class="d">Stop the activity that creates the risk.</span>
                </label>
                <label class="risk-treat-opt">
                    <input type="radio" name="rvTreatment" value="Treat" />
                    <span class="t">Treat / Reduce</span>
                    <span class="d">Another round of controls is required.</span>
                </label>
                <label class="risk-treat-opt">
                    <input type="radio" name="rvTreatment" value="Transfer" />
                    <span class="t">Transfer / Share</span>
                    <span class="d">Insure, outsource or contractually share the exposure.</span>
                </label>
                <label class="risk-treat-opt">
                    <input type="radio" name="rvTreatment" value="Tolerate" />
                    <span class="t">Tolerate / Accept</span>
                    <span class="d">The risk is acceptable as it stands — goes on to acceptance.</span>
                </label>
            </div>
        </section>

        @* ---- Next review date ---------------------------------------- *@
        <section class="pm-panel">
            <div class="pm-section-heading">
                <h2>Next review</h2>
                <p>What you suggest for the next cycle. The person who accepts this risk sees it and can change it.</p>
            </div>
            @* 299. Both fields are PROPOSALS now, not controls. A review
               always sends the risk to Accept Risk (it moves to
               Monitoring), and these two travel with it so the accepter
               starts from the reviewer's suggestion instead of a blank
               form. Before 299 a date here silently kept the risk off
               the Accept tab, and choosing a frequency filled that date
               in -- so the helpful action was the one that broke it. *@
            <div class="pm-form-grid">
                <label>
                    <span>Review frequency</span>
                    <select id="rvReviewFrequency"><option value="">-- select --</option></select>
                    <span class="pm-hint">Fills the date beside it. Both are suggestions.</span>
                </label>
                <label>
                    <span>Next review date</span>
                    <input type="date" id="rvNextReview" />
                    <span class="pm-hint">
                        Optional. Whatever you put here is carried to the acceptance screen
                        as the proposed date &mdash; it does not decide where this risk goes next.
                    </span>
                </label>
            </div>
        </section>

        @* ---- Action bar ----------------------------------------------
           One primary action. Where it lands is decided by the treatment
           option and stated in step 3 of the rail above, so there is no
           second "save and go to..." button to choose between — and no
           step the reviewer has to take by hand afterwards. *@
        <div class="ra-actionbar">
            <p class="pm-form-message" id="rvMessage" role="status" aria-live="polite"></p>
            <div class="ra-actionbar-buttons">
                <button type="button" class="pm-button" id="rvCancelBtn">Cancel</button>
                <button type="submit" class="pm-button primary">
                    <i class="fa-solid fa-floppy-disk"></i> Save review
                </button>
            </div>
        </div>
    </form>
</section>

@* ================= Risk Treatment — FULL PAGE (261, 263) ============
    The treatment task and its sub tasks.

    EVERYTHING HERE IS TASK CENTRE'S, SHOWN IN PLACE.
    The task, its status, its owner, its SLA and its children all belong
    to Task Centre and are read and written through Task Centre's own
    endpoints (practice/api/tasks/{id}/children). This page is a lens on
    that data from the risk's side -- it does not keep its own copy and
    it does not implement a second task mechanism.

    Sub tasks use sp_task_child_create, which is what makes the parent
    refuse to close while a mandatory child is open (BRD §12). That gate
    is the reason residual analysis can trust "all treatment tasks
    closed" to mean the work is genuinely done.

    A PAGE, NOT A MODAL, for the same reason Risk Analysis is: it
    carries a task grid that grows with the work, a sub-task form, and a
    gate notice, and inside an 820px pm-modal-wide the grid loses its
    Due and Sub-task columns to wrapping while the page around it
    scrolls. Same shell as the analysis page -- pm-panel per section,
    pm-section-heading on each, the master layout's container-fluid
    width.
=================================================================== *@

@* ================= Risk Details — READ-ONLY FULL PAGE ==============
    Opened by "View risk" on the register's 3-dot menu. The whole life of
    one risk on one page: context, inherent score, treatment, scope,
    residual score, acceptance.

    READ-ONLY MEANS READ-ONLY. There is not one input, select, textarea
    or save button below, and the loader issues GETs only -- in
    particular it calls refreshTreatmentState with sync:false, because
    the Treatment and Residual pages deliberately POST treatment-sync
    before reading and a page whose whole job is to look at a risk must
    not move it to Monitoring as a side effect of being opened.

    IT REUSES, IT DOES NOT COPY. Every block here is a component the
    Residual page already read-only-renders:
      pm-panel / pm-section-heading      section shell
      risk-flow                          the stage rail
      pm-detail-grid + dd()              label/value lists
      risk-gate, risk-tiles, taskRow()   treatment state, readOnly:true
      riskMapping.mount(..., readOnly)   practices + dependencies
    Nothing on this page has its own renderer where one already exists,
    so a change to any of those shows up here too.

    SECTIONS ARE GATED BY WHAT THE RISK HAS REACHED, not hidden. A risk
    still awaiting analysis has no treatment, residual or acceptance to
    show -- those panels stay in place and say what has to happen first,
    because a section that vanishes reads as a missing feature while a
    section that explains itself reads as a workflow.

    IT IS NOT A NEW SCREEN KEY. Like the other three full pages it is a
    sibling of the tab panels, shown by showFullPage() and left by
    backFromFullPage(), so it needs no menu row, no permission seed and
    no navigation round trip.
=================================================================== *@
<section id="riskDetailPageView" class="risk-page" hidden>
    <div class="pm-page-heading compact ra-head">
        <div>
            <p class="pm-eyebrow">Risk Centre / Risk Register</p>
            <h1 id="rdPageTitle">Risk details</h1>
            <p id="rdPageSubtitle">Everything recorded for this risk, read-only.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="ra-head-actions">
            @* One lifecycle badge, from the derived stage. The second
               span that used to sit here carried the stored record
               status and read as a competing "status". *@
            <span id="rdHeadStage"></span>
            @* Actions (change request, 2026-09-22): the same items the
               Register row menu offers for this risk, gated by the same
               permission and status/applicability rules -- both build
               from buildRegisterMenu() in risk-centre.js, so there is
               exactly one implementation of those rules. "View risk" is
               not offered here: this page already IS that view. *@
            @* class="pm-button" ONLY -- not pm-action-trigger too. That
               class exists for the small square 3-dot triggers on grid
               rows (data-reg-menu etc.) and, defined later in
               practice-management.css with equal specificity, its
               background/border/padding/display:inline-grid win over
               pm-button's for every property they share -- on a button
               this size that reads as the icon, "Actions" and the
               chevron stacking on three lines inside a tiny square
               instead of one bordered button. data-rd-menu alone is
               enough for wireDelegation()'s selector below (no other
               element on this page carries it), so the CSS collision is
               avoided by simply not opting into that class. *@
            <button type="button" class="pm-button" id="rdActionsBtn"
                    data-rd-menu="1" aria-haspopup="true" aria-expanded="false">
                <i class="fa-solid fa-bolt" aria-hidden="true"></i> Actions
                <i class="fa-solid fa-chevron-down" aria-hidden="true" style="font-size:10px; margin-left:2px;"></i>
            </button>
            @* Print, not "Export": the browser's own dialog offers PDF,
               so this needs no server route and no new dependency. *@
            <button type="button" class="pm-button ghost" id="rdPrintBtn">
                <i class="fa-solid fa-print"></i> Print
            </button>
            <button type="button" class="pm-button ghost" id="rdBackBtn">
                <i class="fa-solid fa-arrow-left"></i> Back to register
            </button>
        </div>
    </div>

    @* Where the risk has got to, before any detail. Same .risk-flow the
       Residual page uses for its three-step rail. *@
    <ol class="risk-flow risk-flow-4" id="rdFlow" aria-label="Risk progress"></ol>

    <div class="rd-body">
        <section class="pm-panel">
            <div class="pm-section-heading">
                <h2>Risk context</h2>
                <p>What the risk is, and where it came from.</p>
            </div>
            <dl class="pm-detail-grid" id="rdContext"></dl>
            <h3 class="risk-subhead">Traceability</h3>
            <div id="rdTrace" class="risk-trace"></div>
        </section>

        <section class="pm-panel">
            <div class="pm-section-heading">
                <h2>Inherent risk level <span class="pm-hint">before treatment</span></h2>
                <p>Likelihood &times; impact on this organisation's matrix, as scored at analysis.</p>
            </div>
            <div id="rdInherent"></div>
        </section>

        @* ---- Impact Details -----------------------------------------
           ABOVE Treatment details on this page, not just above Existing
           Controls: the read-only page states the risk in the order it
           was assessed -- context, inherent level, what it impacts, then
           what was done about it. Read-only, like everything else here.
           *@
        <section class="pm-panel" id="rdImpactPanel">
            <div class="pm-section-heading">
                <h2>Impact Details</h2>
                <p>
                    What this risk impacts &mdash; assets, vendors, people, teams and committees,
                    by Operationalize category.
                </p>
            </div>
            <div id="rdImpactScope" class="risk-map-host"></div>
        </section>

        <section class="pm-panel">
            <div class="pm-section-heading">
                <h2>Treatment details</h2>
                <p>The decision taken about this risk, and the work raised to carry it out.</p>
            </div>
            <div id="rdTreatment"></div>
            @* Kept OUT of #rdTreatment: refreshTreatmentState() writes
               into these three by id, so they must exist in the DOM
               before it runs and must not be replaced by the gating
               renderer. rd-when-treated is toggled hidden instead. *@
            <div id="rdTreatmentWork" class="rd-when-treated" hidden>
                <div class="risk-gate" id="rdGate"></div>
                <div class="risk-tiles" id="rdTiles"></div>
                <h3 class="risk-subhead">Treatment tasks <span class="pm-hint">from Task Centre</span></h3>
                <div class="pm-table-wrap">
                    @* Seven columns: taskRow(readOnly:true) omits the
                       Actions column rather than rendering a disabled
                       menu nobody can use. *@
                    <table id="rdTaskTable">
                        <thead>
                            <tr>
                                <th>Task</th><th>Title</th><th>Owner</th><th>Priority</th>
                                <th>Target date</th><th>Status</th><th>Sub tasks</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody id="rdTaskBody">
                            <tr><td colspan="7" class="pm-empty-row">Loading treatment work...</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
            </div>
        </section>

        <section class="pm-panel">
            <div class="pm-section-heading">
                <h2>Existing controls</h2>
                <p>
                    Practices that control this risk and the dependencies each one brings in.
                </p>
            </div>
            @* TWO hosts, ONE mount: this one takes "Mapped practices"
               (each card listing its own dependencies read-only) and
               #rdImpactScope above takes the Impact Details table.
               Mounted read-only, so it draws no Map button, no unmap
               control, no tick boxes and no save bar, and it skips the
               mapping/options fetch entirely. *@
            <div id="rdMapping" class="risk-map-host"></div>
        </section>

        <section class="pm-panel">
            <div class="pm-section-heading">
                <h2>Residual risk level <span class="pm-hint">after treatment</span></h2>
                <p>What is left once the treatment has been carried out, scored on the same matrix.</p>
            </div>
            <div id="rdResidual"></div>
        </section>

        <section class="pm-panel">
            <div class="pm-section-heading">
                <h2>Risk acceptance</h2>
                <p>Who carried this risk forward, on what terms, and when it comes back for review.</p>
            </div>
            <div id="rdAcceptance"></div>
        </section>
    </div>
</section>

<section id="riskTreatmentPageView" class="risk-page" hidden>
    <div class="pm-page-heading compact ra-head">
        <div>
            <p class="pm-eyebrow">Risk Centre / Risk Register</p>
            <h1 id="twPageTitle">Risk treatment</h1>
            <p id="twPageSubtitle">Track the treatment work for this risk and break it into sub tasks.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="ra-head-actions">
            <span id="twHeadStatus"></span>
            <button type="button" class="pm-button ghost" id="twBackBtn">
                <i class="fa-solid fa-arrow-left"></i> Back to register
            </button>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="ra-form">
        <input type="hidden" id="twRiskId" />

        @* ---- Row 1: the risk, beside where the treatment stands ----- *@
        <div class="ra-row ra-row-split">
            <section class="pm-panel">
                <div class="pm-section-heading">
                    <h2>Risk</h2>
                    <p>The risk this treatment work belongs to.</p>
                </div>
                <dl class="pm-detail-grid" id="twMeta"></dl>
            </section>

            <section class="pm-panel">
                <div class="pm-section-heading">
                    <h2>Treatment progress</h2>
                    <p>Residual analysis unlocks once every treatment task is closed.</p>
                </div>
                @* The gate, stated plainly. sp_risk_treatment_state returns
                   both the answer and the reason, so a disabled button can
                   explain itself instead of just being grey. *@
                <div class="risk-gate" id="twGate"></div>
                <div class="risk-tiles" id="twTiles"></div>
            </section>
        </div>

        @* ---- Treatment tasks ---------------------------------------- *@
        <section class="pm-panel">
            <div class="pm-section-heading">
                <h2>Treatment tasks <span class="pm-hint">from Task Center</span></h2>
                <p>
                    Parent tasks own the commitment; sub tasks distribute the work. A parent
                    cannot be completed while a mandatory sub task is still open.
                </p>
            </div>
            <div class="pm-table-wrap">
                <table id="twTable">
                    <thead>
                        <tr>
                            <th>Task</th><th>Title</th><th>Owner</th><th>Priority</th>
                            <th>Due</th><th>Status</th><th>Sub tasks</th><th aria-label="Actions"></th>
                        </tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody id="twTableBody">
                        <tr><td colspan="8" class="pm-empty-row">No treatment work raised.</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>
        </section>

        @* Sub tasks are created through THE common Task / Sub Task form
           (Shared/task-form.js), opened with this risk's treatment task
           fixed as the parent. This page used to carry its own six-field
           panel with its own validation -- a third implementation of one
           concept, alongside the Task Center dialog and its prompt()
           chain. The button below is all that is left of it. *@

        <div class="ra-actionbar">
            <p class="pm-form-message" id="twMessage" role="status" aria-live="polite"></p>
            <div class="ra-actionbar-buttons">
                <button type="button" class="pm-button" id="twCloseBtn">Close</button>
                <button type="button" class="pm-button" id="twAddChildBtn">
                    <i class="fa-solid fa-plus"></i> Add sub task
                </button>
                <button type="button" class="pm-button primary" id="twResidualBtn" disabled>
                    <i class="fa-solid fa-scale-balanced"></i> Residual risk analysis
                </button>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

@* ================= Accept Risk — FULL PAGE ==========================
    Recording the acceptance decision for one risk.

    IT WAS A MODAL, AND THE MODAL WAS THE PROBLEM.
    Acceptance is the last step of a four-step story, and it is the one
    step that is a JUDGEMENT rather than a measurement: somebody signs
    that this level of residual risk is acceptable to the organisation.
    Signing that needs the whole record in front of you — what the risk
    is, what it scored, what it impacts, what was done about it, what is
    left — and an 820px popup could show none of it. What it showed
    instead was five summary lines plus a collapsed block that had to be
    opened, so the decision was made against a summary of a summary.

    THE SAME SHELL AS RESIDUAL ANALYSIS, deliberately: pm-page-heading
    with the risk as the h1, the four-step risk-flow-4 rail, a pm-panel
    per step, a sticky ra-actionbar at the foot. An analyst moving from
    Residual to Accept should not have to learn a second layout for the
    next step of the same story.

    EVERYTHING ABOVE THE LAST SECTION IS READ-ONLY.
    Not disabled — absent. There is no control to press, because
    acceptance records a decision ABOUT what analysis and residual
    assessment found; it does not re-open the finding. The two scope
    hosts are mounted read-only (chips, no pickers), the treatment
    section is rendered by the Treatment page's own renderer with
    readOnly:true and sync:false, and every other value is a dl.

    THE FORM IDS ARE UNCHANGED FROM THE MODAL. acRiskId, acGuidance,
    acMessage, acAcceptedBy, acAcceptedDate, acReviewFrequency,
    acNextReview, acNote and #riskAcceptanceForm are the same elements
    under the same ids, so onAcceptanceSubmit, the frequency-to-date
    rule and every validation message came across untouched. This is a
    re-housing of the decision, not a rewrite of it.

    BULK ACCEPT IS STILL A MODAL, and should be: it asks for the four
    values that are common to twelve risks and shows none of the
    per-risk record, so there is nothing for a page to carry.
=================================================================== *@
<section id="riskAcceptancePageView" class="risk-page" hidden>
    <div class="pm-page-heading compact ra-head">
        <div>
            <p class="pm-eyebrow">Risk Centre / Risk Register</p>
            <h1 id="acPageTitle">Accept risk</h1>
            <p id="acPageSubtitle">Confirm that this level of residual risk is acceptable, and set when it comes back for review.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="ra-head-actions">
            <span id="acHeadStatus"></span>
            <button type="button" class="pm-button ghost" id="acBackBtn">
                <i class="fa-solid fa-arrow-left"></i> Back to register
            </button>
        </div>
    </div>

    @* Step 4 is the current one here, which is the whole point of the
       rail on this page: the three steps behind it carry their real
       values, so "accepting WHAT?" is answered before anything is
       asked. Rendered by renderAcceptanceFlow(). *@
    <ol class="risk-flow risk-flow-4" id="acFlow" aria-label="Inherent risk to treatment to residual risk to acceptance"></ol>

    <form id="riskAcceptanceForm" class="ra-form">
        <input type="hidden" id="acRiskId" />

        @* ---- The risk, as it was ------------------------------------
           Same split as the residual page's step 1: "what the risk is"
           and "what it scored" are two questions. *@
        <div class="ra-row ra-row-split ra-step" data-step="1">
            <section class="pm-panel">
                <div class="pm-section-heading">
                    <h2><span class="ra-step-badge">1</span> Risk summary</h2>
                    <p>What the risk is. Recorded at assessment &mdash; change it from the register.</p>
                </div>
                <dl class="pm-detail-grid" id="acSummary"></dl>
            </section>

            <section class="pm-panel">
                <div class="pm-section-heading">
                    <h2>Inherent risk <span class="pm-hint">before treatment</span></h2>
                    <p>The rating this acceptance is measured from, and the impact analysis behind it.</p>
                </div>
                <dl class="pm-detail-grid" id="acInherent"></dl>
            </section>
        </div>

        @* ---- Impact Details, read-only ------------------------------
           Above the treatment section, as on Residual and View Risk:
           what the risk impacts is the thing an acceptance is a
           decision about. *@
        <section class="pm-panel" id="acImpactPanel">
            <div class="pm-section-heading">
                <h2>Impact Details <span class="pm-hint">read-only</span></h2>
                <p>
                    What this risk impacts &mdash; assets, vendors, people, teams and
                    committees, as established at Risk Analysis.
                </p>
            </div>
            <div id="acImpactScope" class="risk-map-host"></div>
        </section>

        @* ---- What was done about it ---------------------------------
           The Treatment page's own renderer with readOnly:true and
           sync:false. Read-only, and it must not move the risk's status
           as a side effect of being looked at. *@
        <section class="pm-panel ra-step" data-step="2" id="acTreatmentPanel">
            <div class="pm-section-heading ra-heading-row">
                <div>
                    <h2><span class="ra-step-badge">2</span> Risk treatment <span class="pm-hint">read-only</span></h2>
                    <p>The treatment recorded for this risk, exactly as the Risk Treatment page holds it.</p>
                </div>
                <button type="button" class="pm-button ghost" id="acOpenTreatmentBtn">
                    <i class="fa-solid fa-diagram-project"></i> Open risk treatment
                </button>
            </div>

            <div class="risk-gate" id="acTreatmentGate"></div>
            <dl class="pm-detail-grid" id="acTreatmentMeta"></dl>
            <div class="risk-tiles" id="acTiles"></div>

            <h3 class="risk-subhead">Treatment tasks <span class="pm-hint">from Task Center</span></h3>
            <div class="pm-table-wrap">
                @* Seven columns: taskRow() omits the Actions column when
                   called with readOnly:true rather than rendering a
                   disabled menu nobody can use. *@
                <table id="acTaskTable">
                    <thead>
                        <tr>
                            <th>Task</th><th>Title</th><th>Owner</th><th>Priority</th>
                            <th>Target date</th><th>Status</th><th>Sub tasks</th>
                        </tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody id="acTaskBody">
                        <tr><td colspan="7" class="pm-empty-row">Loading treatment work...</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>
        </section>

        @* ---- What is left -------------------------------------------
           The number the acceptance is actually about. Read-only: it is
           scored on the Residual page, and a risk with no residual
           score cannot be accepted at all — which is what the gate in
           step 4 says in words. *@
        <section class="pm-panel ra-step" data-step="3">
            <div class="pm-section-heading ra-heading-row">
                <div>
                    <h2><span class="ra-step-badge">3</span> Residual risk <span class="pm-hint">read-only</span></h2>
                    <p>What the treatment left. This is the level being accepted.</p>
                </div>
                <button type="button" class="pm-button ghost" id="acOpenResidualBtn">
                    <i class="fa-solid fa-scale-balanced"></i> Open residual analysis
                </button>
            </div>
            <dl class="pm-detail-grid" id="acResidual"></dl>
        </section>

        @* ---- Existing controls --------------------------------------
           Mounted read-only into #acMapping, paired with the impact
           host above by ONE riskMapping.mount — one /mapping call for
           both sections. *@
        <section class="pm-panel">
            <div class="pm-section-heading">
                <h2>Existing controls</h2>
                <p>Practices that control this risk and the dependencies each one brings in.</p>
            </div>
            <div id="acMapping" class="risk-map-host"></div>
        </section>

        @* ================= STEP 4 — the decision ======================
           The only editable section on the page, and the only one that
           asks for anything.

           NEXT REVIEW DATE IS REQUIRED AND MUST BE IN THE FUTURE. The
           procedure refuses without it (56601/56602) and the input is
           marked required, but the rule is SQL's — a client-side
           required attribute is an affordance, not a guarantee. The
           reason it is required at all: this column is the only thing
           that ever brings an accepted risk back. Accepted with a NULL
           review date, a risk is invisible to Review Risk and to the
           calendar forever — quietly retired without anybody deciding
           to retire it.

           .ac-grid, the same two-column grid the bulk form uses:

               WHO / WHEN            |   WHY
               Accepted by           |   Acceptance rationale
               Accepted date         |   (spans all four rows, so the
               Review frequency      |    textarea is tall without
               Next review date      |    making the form tall)

           Short answers stacked beside one long one. Review frequency
           (293) sits directly above the date it drives. *@
        <section class="pm-panel ra-step" data-step="4" id="acDecisionPanel">
            <div class="pm-section-heading">
                <h2><span class="ra-step-badge">4</span> Acceptance decision</h2>
                <p>Who accepts this risk, on what date, and when it returns for review.</p>
            </div>

            @* sp_risk_acceptance_get returns both the answer and the
               reason, so this banner explains itself rather than being a
               colour. It is the gate on whether this page can save at
               all. *@
            <div class="risk-gate" id="acGuidance"></div>

            <div class="pm-form-grid ac-grid">
                <label>
                    <span>Accepted by<span class="pm-required">*</span></span>
                    <select id="acAcceptedBy" required><option value="">-- select --</option></select>
                </label>
                <label>
                    <span>Accepted date</span>
                    <input type="date" id="acAcceptedDate" />
                </label>
                @* Migration 293. Directly above the date it drives, so
                   the cause sits above its effect and the pair reads as
                   one decision. Optional: a risk may be given a date with
                   no cadence behind it, which is what every acceptance
                   recorded before 293 has. *@
                <label>
                    <span>Review frequency</span>
                    <select id="acReviewFrequency"><option value="">-- select --</option></select>
                    <span class="pm-hint">Sets the date below. Leave blank, or pick Custom, to choose the date yourself.</span>
                </label>
                <label>
                    <span>Next review date<span class="pm-required">*</span></span>
                    <input type="date" id="acNextReview" required />
                    <span class="pm-hint">Must be in the future &mdash; it is what brings this risk back for review.</span>
                </label>
                @* Explicitly placed in column 2, spanning the four rows
                   the left column occupies. The four fields above are
                   auto-placed and flow down column 1 around it. *@
                <label class="ac-note">
                    <span>Acceptance rationale</span>
                    <textarea id="acNote" rows="3" maxlength="4000"
                              placeholder="Why is this level of risk acceptable to the organisation?"></textarea>
                </label>
            </div>
        </section>

        @* ---- Action bar ----------------------------------------------
           Sticky, like the residual page's: on a page this tall the
           alternative is scrolling to the end to save, then back up to
           read the validation message that stopped it. *@
        <div class="ra-actionbar">
            <p class="pm-form-message" id="acMessage" role="status" aria-live="polite"></p>
            <div class="ra-actionbar-buttons">
                <button type="button" class="pm-button" id="acCancelBtn">Cancel</button>
                <button type="submit" class="pm-button primary">
                    <i class="fa-solid fa-circle-check"></i> Accept risk
                </button>
            </div>
        </div>
    </form>
</section>
@* ================= Bulk accept (migration 295) =====================
    THE SAME FIELDS AS THE SINGLE ACCEPT FORM, MINUS THE PER-RISK ONES.

    Accepted by, accepted date, review frequency, next review date and
    the rationale are all shared judgements -- "the committee accepted
    these twelve on Tuesday, next review in a year" is one decision about
    twelve risks, not twelve decisions.

    What is NOT here is the per-risk context the single form shows: the
    ratings, the treatment option, the acceptGuidance banner. Those
    differ per risk and cannot be summarised into one panel honestly, so
    the tab shows them as columns in the grid instead and this form asks
    only for what is genuinely common.

    NOT bulk review with Status = Accepted. That stamps a review
    (last_reviewed_dt, review_count) which a first acceptance has not
    earned. Both call sp_risk_acceptance_save, so the rules are shared
    and only the review stamp differs -- see 295's header.
=================================================================== *@
<div class="pm-modal" id="riskBulkAcceptModal" hidden>
    <div class="pm-modal-panel pm-modal-wide risk-accept-panel">
        <header>
            <h2>Accept selected risks</h2>
            <button class="pm-modal-close" type="button" data-close-bulk-accept aria-label="Close">&times;</button>
        </header>
        <form id="riskBulkAcceptForm" data-session-employee-id="@sessionEmployeeId">
            <p class="pm-hint" id="baSubject" style="margin:0 0 10px;"></p>

            <div class="pm-form-grid ac-grid">
                <label>
                    <span>Accepted by<span class="pm-required">*</span></span>
                    <select id="baAcceptedBy" required><option value="">-- select --</option></select>
                    <span class="pm-hint">Recorded on every selected risk. Anyone from another organisation is refused.</span>
                </label>
                <label>
                    <span>Accepted date</span>
                    <input type="date" id="baAcceptedDate" />
                </label>
                <label>
                    <span>Review frequency</span>
                    <select id="baReviewFrequency"><option value="">-- select --</option></select>
                    <span class="pm-hint">Sets the date below. Leave blank, or pick Custom, to choose it yourself.</span>
                </label>
                <label>
                    <span>Next review date<span class="pm-required">*</span></span>
                    <input type="date" id="baNextReview" required />
                    <span class="pm-hint">Must be in the future &mdash; it is what brings these risks back for review.</span>
                </label>
                <label class="ac-note">
                    <span>Acceptance rationale</span>
                    <textarea id="baNote" rows="3" maxlength="4000"
                              placeholder="Why is this level of risk acceptable to the organisation?"></textarea>
                </label>
            </div>

            @* Filled after a save: one line per risk, so a partial result
               is visible rather than averaged into "saved". *@
            <div id="baOutcome" style="margin-top:10px;"></div>

            <p class="pm-form-message" id="baMessage" role="status" aria-live="polite"></p>
            <footer class="pm-modal-footer">
                <button type="button" class="pm-button" data-close-bulk-accept>Cancel</button>
                <button type="submit" class="pm-button primary" id="baSubmit">
                    <i class="fa-solid fa-circle-check"></i> Accept risks
                </button>
            </footer>
        </form>
    </div>
</div>

@* ============ Bulk review (migrations 270, 294) ====================
    SHARED DISPOSITIONS ONLY. Everything else about a risk is a per-risk
    judgement: likelihood, impact, owner, treatment option. One value
    applied to thirty risks would be a fabricated assessment, which is
    why this is NOT the single-risk Review over a list -- that one
    re-scores through sp_risk_register_assess and requires likelihood and
    impact per risk.

    294 added Review frequency, and it belongs to this set for the same
    reason the date does: a cadence is a SCHEDULING decision that is
    genuinely the same for every risk in a quarterly sweep, not an
    assessment of any one of them. The test for admitting a field here is
    unchanged -- would one value across thirty risks be a fact or a
    fabrication?

    299 REMOVED the Status picker, which fails that test from the other
    direction. A bulk review already says what it is: the procedure moves
    every reviewed risk to Monitoring, and that is what sends it to the
    Accept tab. A status box on top of that let one action contradict
    itself -- and did, silently.

    So this form is now three fields, and all three are the same kind of
    thing: what happened (description), and what the reviewer SUGGESTS
    for next time (frequency, date). Neither suggestion decides anything;
    both travel to the Accept screen for whoever accepts to confirm or
    change.

    This records a review DISPOSITION: it was looked at, here is the
    note, here is where it stands, here is when to look again.

    Description writes the REVIEW's remark and the audit trail -- not
    each risk's risk_description, which is what the risk IS and differs
    per risk. Applying one description to five risks would erase five
    different descriptions.

    Status is not written directly. sp_risk_bulk_review routes it through
    sp_risk_acceptance_save or sp_risk_register_status_set, so a risk
    without a treatment option or a completed analysis is SKIPPED and
    reported rather than forced into a state the rest of the product
    believes cannot exist.
=================================================================== *@
<div class="pm-modal" id="riskBulkReviewModal" hidden>
    <div class="pm-modal-panel">
        <header>
            <h2>Bulk review</h2>
            <button class="pm-modal-close" type="button" data-close-bulk-review aria-label="Close">&times;</button>
        </header>
        <form id="riskBulkReviewForm">
            <p class="pm-hint" id="brSubject" style="margin:0 0 10px;"></p>

            <div class="pm-form-grid">
                <label class="pm-form-grid-full">
                    <span>Description</span>
                    <textarea id="brRemarks" rows="3" maxlength="4000"
                              placeholder="e.g. Reviewed as part of quarterly risk review"></textarea>
                    <span class="pm-hint">
                        Recorded as the review note on every selected risk and on its audit trail.
                        It does not overwrite each risk's own description.
                    </span>
                </label>

                @* THE STATUS PICKER IS GONE (299).
                   A bulk review means one thing -- "these were looked at"
                   -- and the procedure now says it in the status by
                   moving them to Monitoring, which is what puts them on
                   the Accept tab. Offering a status box on top of that
                   was a second way to say the same thing, and it went
                   wrong in both directions: picking Accepted on an
                   already-accepted risk silently did nothing, and picking
                   anything at all stopped the review reaching acceptance.

                   Accepting in bulk has its own place now -- the Accept
                   Risk tab's "Accept selected" (295) -- so nothing was
                   lost by removing it. Forcing some other status is a
                   per-risk decision and belongs on the risk itself. *@

                @* Migration 294. Same control, same placement and the
                   same derivation rule as the single Accept form, so a
                   reviewer meets one behaviour rather than two. With
                   Status = Accepted it reaches sp_risk_acceptance_save
                   and is recorded on every accepted risk; otherwise it
                   is stamped alongside the review date. *@
                <label>
                    <span>Review frequency</span>
                    <select id="brReviewFrequency"><option value="">-- leave unchanged --</option></select>
                    <span class="pm-hint">
                        Suggested cadence. Fills the date below and travels with it to
                        Accept Risk. Leave blank, or pick Custom, to choose the date yourself.
                    </span>
                </label>

                @* 299 replaced 297's rule. This date no longer decides
                   anything: a bulk review always sends its risks to
                   Accept Risk (they move to Monitoring), and the date is
                   carried there as the reviewer's suggestion. Which is
                   what makes the frequency select above safe -- under
                   297 it filled this box and thereby stopped the risk
                   ever reaching acceptance. *@
                <label>
                    <span>Next review date</span>
                    <input type="date" id="brNextReview" />
                    <span class="pm-hint">
                        Suggested date, carried to <strong>Accept Risk</strong> for whoever accepts
                        these &mdash; they can change it. Must be in the future.
                    </span>
                </label>
            </div>

            @* Filled after a save: one line per risk, so a partial result
               is visible rather than averaged into "saved". *@
            <div id="brOutcome" style="margin-top:10px;"></div>

            <p class="pm-form-message" id="brMessage" role="status" aria-live="polite"></p>
            <footer class="pm-modal-footer">
                <button type="button" class="pm-button" data-close-bulk-review>Cancel</button>
                <button type="submit" class="pm-button primary" id="brSubmit">
                    <i class="fa-solid fa-check"></i> Apply to selected
                </button>
            </footer>
        </form>
    </div>
</div>

@* ================= Reassign treatment task =========================
    POSTs to the EXISTING /practice/api/tasks/{id}/assign — the same
    endpoint and the same sp_task_assign Task Center has always used. No
    API and no schema change: reassignment already existed, it just had
    no way in from this page.

    WHY A PICKER AND NOT A prompt()
    -------------------------------
    Task Center's own Reassign is `prompt()` asking the operator to read
    an employee id out of a printed list and type it back. That is the
    same interaction the common Task/Sub Task form just replaced for Add
    Child Task, and copying it here would spread it rather than retire
    it. state.employees is already loaded on this page for the acceptance
    and owner selects, so the list costs nothing.

    The reason is optional but goes to sp_task_assign's @reason_text, so
    the audit_trail row it writes says WHY the owner changed, not just
    that it did.
=================================================================== *@
<div class="pm-modal" id="riskTaskAssignModal" hidden>
    <div class="pm-modal-panel">
        <header>
            <h2>Reassign treatment task</h2>
            <button class="pm-modal-close" type="button" data-close-task-assign aria-label="Close">&times;</button>
        </header>
        <form id="riskTaskAssignForm">
            <input type="hidden" id="taTaskId" />
            <dl class="risk-meta" id="taMeta"></dl>
            <div class="pm-form-grid">
                <label class="pm-form-grid-full">
                    <span>Reassign to<span class="pm-required">*</span></span>
                    <select id="taAssignee" required><option value="">-- select --</option></select>
                    <span class="pm-hint">
                        The task keeps its priority, SLA and sub tasks — only the owner changes.
                    </span>
                </label>
                <label class="pm-form-grid-full">
                    <span>Reason</span>
                    <textarea id="taReason" rows="2" maxlength="500"
                              placeholder="Recorded on the task's audit trail."></textarea>
                </label>
            </div>
            <p class="pm-form-message" id="taMessage" role="status" aria-live="polite"></p>
            <footer class="pm-modal-footer">
                <button type="button" class="pm-button" data-close-task-assign>Cancel</button>
                <button type="submit" class="pm-button primary">
                    <i class="fa-solid fa-user-pen"></i> Reassign
                </button>
            </footer>
        </form>
    </div>
</div>

@* ================= Stage-2 approval modal (BRD §19) ================= *@
<div class="pm-modal" id="riskRegApprovalModal" hidden>
    <div class="pm-modal-panel pm-modal-wide">
